Section 4. The 3-colouring c_0 : R^2 -> {1,2,3} (circle 2-coloured by parity of
           floor(3 arg / pi), off-circle = 3) satisfies the four axioms at 0
========================================================================================
PASS  4.1 e^{i(theta-pi/3)} + e^{i(theta+pi/3)} = e^{i theta} (equilateral case)   [2cos(pi/3)-1 = 0]
PASS  4.2 |e+v|^2 = 2 + 2 cos(theta-phi), so |e+v| = 1 <=> cos(delta) = -1/2
PASS  4.3 at delta = 2pi/3 the modulus is 1 and the argument is (theta+phi)/2
PASS  4.4 floor(3(theta+pi/3)/pi) = floor(3theta/pi) + 1, so R flips the parity
PASS  4.5 exact grid: c_0(e) != c_0(Re) for all 600 rational directions (5.7 at 0)
PASS  4.6 exact grid: in the equilateral case both directions have parity opposite to e+v (5.2)
PASS  4.7 (SA) at 0: c_0(0) = 3 and the palettes on the circle are {1,2}
PASS  4.8 (T) at 0: c_0(e) in {c_0(e)} trivially (singleton palettes)
PASS  4.9 |e - Re| = |1 - e^{i pi/3}| = 1 (the paper's |1-R| = 1), exact
PASS  4.10 {0, e, Re} is a unit triangle, so chi(H_0) >= 3
PASS  4.11 chord of length 1 on S^1 subtends pi/3: 2 sin(delta/2) = 1 <=> delta = pi/3
PASS  4.12 c_0 is proper on H_0 (circle 2-coloured by parity, everything else colour 3)

